# Fifteen!
Tamil title: பதினைந்து!

The fifteenth: one month has passed.

Those who lost hands, those who lost legs, and those whose bodies were pierced: there they stand before us!

The cries raised in six families by the horror of bullets have not ceased. Thousands upon thousands of comrades are, there, continuing their procession into prison.

Prison continues to receive many comrades bearing the marks of baton blows and bullets.

Agony spreads! The strange durbar has not ended!

The government that, on July 15, felled the heroes who raised the flag of liberation for the soil of the motherland, turned the country into a forest of blood, locked treasures of thought inside prisons, and tortured them, celebrates August 15! It hoists the flag amid songs of jubilation, a shower of speeches, and radio anthems.

They say six years have passed since freedom blossomed! The people who lovingly welcomed August 15 and rejoiced that a life of happiness had dawned are today pierced by its bullets and struck by its injustice, crying, "Alas!"

But the government celebrates August 15.

There is no light in homes! There is no peace in anyone's heart! There is no joy; the darkness of sorrow is everywhere. The people who rejoiced six years ago that the white ruler's government had ended now writhe, wail, and scream. The poor man of this country laments, "The white man has gone, but the flag of a government of plunderers flies."

The national flag will fly at Congress meetings today. Will the hearts of the nationalist friends who gaze up at it possess courage? How much blood they shed to obtain August 15! Today all of it has been reduced to this so easily! Explosions will be heard in many places today. But the citizen, accustomed to hearing gunfire again and again, hears the celebratory explosions of freedom and, instead of rejoicing, cries out, hides, and runs away shouting, "Alas!"

To that extent, six years of self-rule have thrown the land into turmoil! The one who ought to enjoy happiness is submerged in sorrow. The government has not provided a life of well-being; it shoots and scorches!

When people ask for life, it gives them bullets! How dreadful is this sight? How much hope this fifteenth carried with it six years ago!

How much cruelty this fifteenth revealed last month!

When the date of liberation returns each year, everyone rejoices in the happiness that foreign tyranny ended on that day.

But here, the number 15 recalls "massacre": a mother raising a cry of grief, maidens wailing, families lamenting, and scenes of cruelty race before our eyes.

How immense is this horror! When the government of liberation for the Indian subcontinent assumed the sceptre in the middle of August, it began under Acharya's leadership. That same Acharya has today given us "July 15."

When Acharya of this subcontinent took the seat once occupied by the white man, countless people rejoiced; we too had a place among them.

But today that same Acharya heaps up calamities surpassing even the white man, and countless people suffer. We have been given first place among them.

August 15 is a good day. But caught in the hands of destroyers, it has lost its light and been debased.

July 15 is a day of sacrifice. It shines in the heroic hearts of an awakened Dravidam. The cruelties committed that day cannot be forgotten as long as we live. They will remain in memory even after the motherland attains liberation!

It was only after such "July fifteenths" that the nationalist friends obtained "August 15."

But they have brought disgrace upon the greatness of the freedom they won; they have stained the independence they obtained.

The Goddess of Freedom sheds tears. Her heart, which ought to rejoice, sheds tears of blood because of the conduct of the destroyers.

She looks upon the martyrs! In her eyes, "August 15" disappears and "July 15" appears. She walks toward the victorious heroes who entered the path of sacrifice and raised a heroic cry on that new day.

The Goddess of Freedom, grieved to see that those who once adored her have become schemers, has turned away. She is proceeding toward the camp of the martyrs! As her stride grows stronger and stronger, the prison doors open and close. Precious comrades in the movement are carried away and remain locked inside. As the Goddess of Freedom draws nearer, those who wield domination repeatedly open and close the prison doors. Today's scene explains it: August 15 passes without heartfelt joy.

Let us take this to heart: after "July," "August" is certain.

After the frenzy of repression, victory is assured.

Let us walk toward that "day" which will bring us joy!

Let injustice perish! Let a government of love blossom!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 16-8-1953)

---NOTES---
The source title begins with the stray OCR string “45,” before “பதினைந்து!”; the clean Tamil title is supplied only as display metadata, while the source remains unchanged.
The final occurrence OCRed as “ஆகஸ்டு 135” is read contextually as “ஆகஸ்டு 15” in translation.
The opening “15- ஒரு மாதம்” and several words elsewhere are visibly OCR-damaged. The translation follows the essay’s recurring July 15/August 15 contrast without silently repairing the Tamil source.
The essay’s historical and political assertions are translated in the source’s voice and are not independently verified here.
